          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>In the pathogenesis of atherosclerosis, what is the earliest visible lesion form</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>In the pathogenesis of atherosclerosis, what is the earliest visible lesion formed when macrophages accumulate oxidised LDL?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Fibrous plaque</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>A fibrous plaque develops later, when smooth muscle proliferation and collagen deposition form a protective cap over the lipid core. The earliest visible lesion precedes this stage.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Fatty streak</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Foam cells (lipid-laden macrophages) accumulate beneath the endothelium to form fatty streaks — the first grossly visible sign of atherosclerosis.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Calcified plaque</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Calcification is a feature of advanced, long-standing plaques. The earliest lesion is much simpler — just collections of foam cells beneath the intima.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Mural thrombus</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Thrombus formation occurs as a complication when a mature plaque ruptures, not as the initial lesion of atherosclerosis.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The correct answer is fatty streak. Macrophages engulf oxidised LDL via scavenger receptors, becoming foam cells. These aggregate beneath the endothelium to form fatty streaks — the first visible step in atherosclerosis."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Fatty streaks are the earliest visible lesion — formed by foam cell accumulation. This stage is potentially reversible with lipid lowering, which is why early intervention matters."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
