          "label": "<p>A 32-year-old woman with familial hypercholesterolaemia in her first trimester n</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A 32-year-old woman with familial hypercholesterolaemia in her first trimester needs lipid-lowering therapy. Which drug class is the most appropriate choice?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>High-intensity statin</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Statins are contraindicated in pregnancy because cholesterol is essential for foetal development, including cell membrane and hormone synthesis.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>PCSK9 inhibitor</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>PCSK9 inhibitors are monoclonal antibodies reserved for refractory cases and are not established as safe in pregnancy.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Bile-acid sequestrant</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Bile-acid sequestrants are not absorbed systemically — they remain in the gut, binding bile acids and promoting hepatic LDL receptor upregulation without foetal exposure.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Ezetimibe</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Ezetimibe crosses the placenta and lacks sufficient safety data in pregnancy. Bile-acid sequestrants are preferred because they are not absorbed systemically.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The correct answer is bile-acid sequestrant. Since these drugs act entirely within the gut lumen and are not absorbed systemically, they do not cross the placenta, making them the safest choice when a pregnant patient requires lipid-lowering therapy."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Bile-acid sequestrants are the safest lipid-lowering option in pregnancy because they are not absorbed from the gut and pose no risk to the foetus."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
                "enableRetry": true,
                "enableSolutionsButton": true,
