          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>Which anti-anginal drug reduces angina frequency without altering heart rate or blood pressure?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Atenolol</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Beta-blockers like atenolol reduce angina specifically by slowing heart rate and reducing contractility, thereby lowering myocardial oxygen demand — clear haemodynamic effects.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Glyceryl trinitrate</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>GTN is a nitrate that causes venodilation, reducing preload and blood pressure. Its haemodynamic effects are central to how it relieves angina.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Ranolazine</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Ranolazine blocks the late sodium current in cardiomyocytes, reducing intracellular calcium overload and diastolic wall tension — without affecting heart rate or blood pressure.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Verapamil</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Verapamil is a rate-limiting calcium channel blocker that slows heart rate and reduces blood pressure, which is how it relieves angina.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The correct answer is ranolazine. Unlike nitrates, beta-blockers, and calcium channel blockers — all of which alter heart rate or blood pressure — ranolazine reduces angina by blocking the late sodium current in cardiomyocytes, reducing calcium overload without haemodynamic changes."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Ranolazine acts via late Na⁺ channel inhibition, a mechanism entirely separate from haemodynamic effects. This makes it valuable as add-on therapy when other agents are inadequate."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
