          "label": "<p>A 45-year-old woman with atrial fibrillation secondary to rheumatic mitral steno</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A 45-year-old woman with atrial fibrillation secondary to rheumatic mitral stenosis needs long-term anticoagulation for stroke prevention. Which is the most appropriate drug?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Apixaban</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>DOACs like apixaban are first-line for non-valvular AF. However, in rheumatic mitral stenosis, DOACs have shown inferior outcomes and are not recommended.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Warfarin</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Warfarin remains the only proven anticoagulant for AF with rheumatic mitral stenosis or mechanical heart valves, where DOACs have shown inferior outcomes.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Dabigatran</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Dabigatran is a direct thrombin inhibitor approved for non-valvular AF. In valvular AF due to rheumatic heart disease, it is not recommended — warfarin is the standard.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Enoxaparin</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>LMWH is used for short-term anticoagulation (e.g., bridging or pregnancy), not for long-term stroke prevention in atrial fibrillation.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The correct answer is warfarin. Despite DOACs being first-line for non-valvular AF, warfarin remains mandatory for AF with rheumatic mitral stenosis or mechanical heart valves, since DOACs have shown inferior outcomes in these specific populations."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Warfarin is the only anticoagulant with proven efficacy in valvular AF (rheumatic mitral stenosis and mechanical valves). As the narration notes — newer is not always better; context determines the right choice."
                }
              ],
